  "text": "Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB M.2 NVMe SSD Review - ServeTheHome Facebook Linkedin RSS TikTok X Youtube Forums AI Server Server Systems Server CPUs Accelerators Server Motherboards Server Chassis Other Components 5G Edge Storage Networking Workstation Workstation Processors Workstation Motherboards Software Operating Systems Server Applications Virtualization Guides Buyer’s Guides Tips Top Hardware Components for TrueNAS / FreeNAS NAS Servers Top Hardware Components for pfSense Appliances Top Hardware Components for napp-it and Solarish NAS Servers Top Picks for Windows Server 2016 Essentials Hardware The DIY WordPress Hosting Server Hardware Guide Search Facebook Linkedin RSS TikTok X Youtube Tuesday, November 10, 2026 ServeTheHome News Storage Reliability Raid Calculator RAID Reliability Calculator | Simple MTTDL Model About Contact Editorial and Copyright Policies AG Substack AG Reports Subscribe ServeTheHome Forums Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Privacy Policy Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. ServeTheHome Advertisement Forums AI Server Server Systems Server CPUs Accelerators Server Motherboards Server Chassis Other Components 5G Edge Storage Networking Workstation Workstation Processors Workstation Motherboards Software Operating Systems Server Applications Virtualization Guides Buyer’s Guides Tips Top Hardware Components for TrueNAS / FreeNAS NAS Servers Top Hardware Components for pfSense Appliances Top Hardware Components for napp-it and Solarish NAS Servers Top Picks for Windows Server 2016 Essentials Hardware The DIY WordPress Hosting Server Hardware Guide Home Storage Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB M.2 NVMe SSD Review Storage Facebook X Pinterest Linkedin ReddIt Email Print Copy URL Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB Today, we are looking at the Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB NVMe SSD. Just over a year ago I took a look at a 2230 sized drive in Lexar’s PLAY line, and today we have a more standard 2280 sized entry. Despite sharing the PLAY branding, this drive and that are very different, in more ways than just size, as you will see in my review. If you want to check the current pricing or want to pick up a drive, you can find the Amazon Affiliate link here. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB PCIe Gen4 NVMe SSD Beneath the included heatsink, the Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB comes in a single-sided M.2 2280 (80mm) form factor. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB Front It has been a while since I have had the opportunity to complain about a drive with a difficult or impossible to remove factory heatsink; thankfully, the streak continues! The included heatsink on the PLAY 2280 has four teeny tiny screws holding it on, but after removing those it pops right off. The NAND appears to be YMTC 232-layer TLC, and the controller is a Maxio MAP1602. That combination of components makes the PLAY essentially identical to the Lexar NM790 I previously reviewed, which makes sense because they have very similar performance claims. Regardless, there is no DRAM cache. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB Back The backside of the Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB has nothing of note, so the bottom of the heatsink is more interesting. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB SSD Specs The Lexar PLAY 2280 drives are available between 2GB and 8TB capacity points. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB Specs All the drives in the PLAY 2280 series come rated for 7400 MB/s read and 6500 MB/s write, and those are big claims for a DRAM-less drive, pitting the PLAY 2280 against the best that Gen 4 has to offer. Endurance is rated at 3200 TBW for the 4TB drive, which is great. My standard for endurance on a 4TB drive would normally be 2400 TBW, and the PLAY 2280 easily exceeds that. The warranty is the industry standard 5-years for a premium drive. Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB CrystalDiskInfo CrystalDiskInfo can give us some basic information about the SSD and confirms we are operating at PCIe 4.0 x4 speeds using NVMe 2.0. Test System Configuration We are using the following configuration for this test: Motherboard : MSI MAG X670E Tomahawk CPU : AMD Ryzen 9 7900X (12C/24T) RAM : 2x 16GB DDR5-6000 UDIMMs Our testing uses the Lexar PLAY 2280 4TB as the boot drive for the system, installed in the M.2_1 slot on the motherboard. This slot supports up to PCIe Gen 5 x4. The drive is filled to 85% capacity with data, and then some is deleted, leaving around 60% used space on the volume. Next, we are going to get into our performance testing. RELATED ARTICLES MORE FROM AUTHOR ADT R27A-BK3 U.2 to PCIe Adapter Review Kioxia LD4 E1.L QLC NVMe SSD Announced Kioxia CM9-R 15.36TB E3.S NVMe SSD Review 1 COMMENT Really wish you’d add power usage to M.2 SSDs. Comments are closed. Newsletter Get the best of STH delivered weekly to your inbox. We are going to curate a selection of the best posts from STH each week and deliver them directly to you. Your email address: By opting-in you agree to have us send you our newsletter. We are using a third party service to manage subscriptions so you can unsubscribe at any time. Leave this field empty if you're human: --- Advertisements --- ABOUT US ServeTheHome is the IT professional's guide to servers, storage, networking, and high-end workstation hardware, plus great open source projects. Advertise on STH DISCLAIMERS: We are a participant in the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program, an affiliate advertising program designed to provide a means for us to earn fees by linking to Amazon.com and affiliated sites. Please be advised of our data usage for machine learning and AI inference and training purposes which can be found here along with our other editorial and copyright policies. FOLLOW US Facebook Linkedin RSS TikTok X Youtube © 2009-2026 Axautik Group LLC",
